# CODEOWNERS
#
# OWNERSHIP HERE IS WHO REVIEWS, NOT WHO MAY EDIT. Changed 2026-09-04 by issue
# #180, and it is a change of meaning rather than of lines: every path below
# reads the same as it did the day before.
#
# When a pull request changes a file, GitHub asks that file's owner to review
# it. That is ALL this file does, and now it is all it is meant to do. There is
# one team, anyone may edit anything, and what stops a change is
# docs/workflow/hard-stops.md, which stops everyone equally. This file no
# longer says where a boundary is, because there is no longer a boundary for it
# to describe.
#
# It has no teeth either way: branch protection is unavailable on the current
# plan, and requiring code-owner review is what would give it force. The
# controls that actually protect main are the local gate, the reviewer agent
# the author runs before the pull request exists, and Hassan reading the diff
# before he merges.
#
# THE LAST MATCHING LINE WINS. Not the most specific one. Order matters.
# A leading / anchors at the repository root. A trailing / matches everything
# inside the directory.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Default. A change anywhere not named below asks Hassan to read it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*                          @zhssnym

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What the user sees.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Both names, so that whoever did not write it is the one asked to read it.
# With Imran alone, every client fix Hassan makes would sit waiting for Imran,
# and most recent client commits were Hassan's.
#
# Component tests live in here too, beside the components they test, and are
# read under this line. That is why app/tests/ is not split.
/app/src/client/           @mranwafa @zhssnym

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The system, the suite, the specification and the infrastructure.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Redundant with the `*` line today. They stop being redundant the moment
# anyone adds a broader rule above them, which is the whole reason to write
# them down. Naming Hassan here asks him to read a change; it does not reserve
# the files, and Imran's work reaches every one of them.
/app/src/server/           @zhssnym
/app/db/                   @zhssnym
/app/tests/                @zhssnym
/.github/                  @zhssnym
/docs/spec/                @zhssnym
/docs/rfcs/                @zhssnym
/docs/workflow/            @zhssnym
/CODEOWNERS                @zhssnym

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The seam.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The contract both halves import. A change here is a change to spec section 7
# in disguise, so it is worth a second reader every time.
#
# A READ schema for a shape the server already sends is an ordinary change:
# nothing on the server imports it, and a wrong one fails loudly on one screen.
# A REQUEST field is a hard stop and never moves alone, because one the server
# does not read is accepted and silently dropped with a 200.
# docs/workflow/hard-stops.md carries the rule.
/app/src/shared/           @zhssnym

# Dependencies. Adding a library is a bundle size, licence and supply chain
# decision, which is a technical ruling and so is read here every time.
/app/package.json          @zhssnym
/app/package-lock.json     @zhssnym
